<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Email from './lib/Email.abide'
import Seats from './lib/Seats.abide'
import InvoiceSearch from './lib/InvoiceSearch.abide'
import PastedRow from './lib/PastedRow.abide'
import InvoiceKey from './lib/InvoiceKey.abide'
import emailSource from './lib/Email.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import seatsSource from './lib/Seats.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import invoiceSearchSource from './lib/InvoiceSearch.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import pastedRowSource from './lib/PastedRow.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import invoiceKeySource from './lib/InvoiceKey.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>Here the annotation you already wrote <strong>is</strong> the schema.
    <code>{'GET(({ id }: { id: number }) => …)'}</code> builds the runtime schema for
    <code>{'{ id: number }'}</code>, and a caller sending <code>{'{ id: "abc" }'}</code> gets a 422
    before the handler runs. Where that derivation is not enough, an option pins the shape, on the
    value rather than at the address.</p>

    <Section title="A derived schema reads `required` off the syntax">
        <p>One rule, readable from the declaration alone: <code>required</code> is which
        destructured members lack defaults, and <code>Args</code> itself is optional where the
        parameter has one.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Declaration</th><th>Schema</th><th>Called as</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'({ page }: { page: number })'}</code></td><td><code>{'{ page: number }'}</code>, <code>page</code> required</td><td><code>{'listPayments({ page: 2 })'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'({ page = 1 })'}</code></td><td><code>{'{ page?: number }'}</code>, <code>default: 1</code></td><td><code>listPayments()</code> or <code>{'listPayments({ page: 7 })'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'({ page = 1 } = {})'}</code></td><td><code>Args</code> itself optional</td><td><code>listPayments()</code> with a bare URL</td></tr>
                <tr><td>no parameter at all</td><td>none</td><td><code>listPayments()</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`args` and `schema` gate what comes in, `transform` shapes what is stored">
        <p>Deriving from an annotation means the build resolves types, so build time is typecheck
        time — and a schema is only ever as good as the inference that reached it. Where inference
        <strong>fails</strong> the compiler reads on rather than refusing the file, because valid
        TypeScript has to compile. The cost is that an unresolved annotation degrades to a
        <strong>wider</strong> schema, silently.</p>

        <p>That is the trade "the shape is never declared twice" is bought with, and the repair is
        an option at each <strong>position</strong>. <code>args</code> gates the arguments a keyed
        value is called with:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="src/invoices/createInvoice.ts — excerpt" code={`import { z } from 'zod'

const invoices = reactive(
    (args: { lines: Line[] }) => database.invoice.create(args.lines),
    { args: z.object({ lines: z.array(lineSchema).min(1) }) },
)

export default POST(invoices)`} />

        <p><code>transform</code> shapes what gets stored:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="src/lib/ledger.ts — excerpt" code={`const paidIds = reactive(() => listInvoices({ year }), {
    transform: (all) => new Set(all.filter((r) => r.paid).map((r) => r.id)),
})`} />

        <p>They are not two spellings of one job. <code>Accepted</code> and <code>Stored</code>
        already name the two ends, and that second one has no validation in it at all. So the
        <strong>option name</strong> is the discriminant: a function in <code>schema</code> or
        <code>args</code> refuses by throwing, a function in <code>transform</code> refuses by
        returning a <code>Failed</code>, and neither has to be told from the other.</p>

        <p><strong>Each gate checks one position.</strong> <code>schema</code> checks the value:
        what a <code>set</code> writes, what a body produced, and what a stream has accumulated
        (4.3, 11.46, 9.8). A <code>patch</code> mutates the value in place and runs no
        <code>schema</code> (5.7), so a write that has to be checked is a <code>set</code>.
        <code>args</code> checks the arguments a keyed value is called with (11.40), and it only
        refuses (11.28). A value whose body takes no parameter has no arguments, so it cannot spell
        <code>args</code> at all (11.41), rather than carrying an option that quietly does
        nothing.</p>

        <p>With no <code>transform</code>, <code>Stored</code> is <code>Accepted</code> and
        <code>schema</code> decides both ends. Nothing declares that; it falls out of the types.</p>

        <p>Either option pins a shape you will not have widened, and either is where a constraint
        the type system cannot express goes — a non-empty array, an ISO date, a bounded number.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A schema runs where its value lives">
        <Example
            id="schema-browser"
            title={'`reactive(v, { schema })` — a check with no server in it'}
            summary="A contact's email. Take the dot out of the domain, and watch the record rather than the field."
            files={{
                'lib/Email.abide': emailSource,
            }}
        >
            <Email />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 option, 0 validators</em> — the arm spells the shape a second time, in a form
        nothing compares against the annotation it came from.</p>

        <p>Not "on the server" — <strong>where the <code>Reactive</code> is declared</strong>. One
        in a page checks in the browser, one in a <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> checks on the
        server, and one in a module both sides import checks wherever it was used. That is where the
        code runs, rather than a rule of validation's own.</p>

        <p>A refused write fills <code>error()</code> rather than throwing, and stores nothing — so
        the field keeps what was typed while the record keeps what it had. On a primitive the issues
        are a bare <code>string[]</code>, there being no path to key them under.</p>

        <p>An rpc's handler is a <code>reactive</code> in a <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code>, so a
        browser calling it through the generated wrapper cannot pre-check — the wrapper carries a
        method, an address and a description, and nothing else. Not an exception; that is where the
        value is. Want the same shape checked on both sides and you put it in a module both import
        and declare it twice — the one duplication this design asks for, and it asks for it
        deliberately.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="There is no `schemas` on `GET` or `POST`">
        <p>A shape is a fact about the <strong>value</strong>, the way <code>ttl</code> is, so it
        builds the value rather than the address. Hand <code>GET</code> a plain function and
        <code>args</code> sits beside <code>description</code> in one object, because <code>GET(fn,
        options)</code> <strong>is</strong> <code>GET(reactive(fn, options))</code> — the
        <code>args</code> gate builds the value, and <code>description</code>,
        <code>middleware</code>, <code>timeout</code>, <code>crossOrigin</code> and
        <code>maxBodySize</code> describe the address.</p>

        <p>So one <code>reactive</code> handed to both <code>GET</code> and <code>POST</code> states
        the shape once between them, and an in-process caller gets the check a wire caller gets. A
        schema declared at the transport is one that <strong>skips itself</strong> every other way
        the handler is reached — read inside another value, imported by a second server module — so
        the shape would hold for your untrusted callers and not for your own code.</p>

        <p>Write the <code>reactive</code> out when something besides the handler holds the value —
        a second handler, a page reading it in process — and pass <code>GET</code> the options
        otherwise.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`Schema<T>` takes two forms">
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`type Schema<T> = ((value: unknown) => T) | StandardSchemaV1<T>`} />

        <p>A schema <strong>returns what it accepts</strong>, so it normalizes as well as refuses.
        That is why the function form is <code>=&gt; T</code> and not a predicate: a
        <code>boolean</code> cannot normalize, and a <code>false</code> stored as the value is the
        silent version of a refusal.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>(value: unknown) =&gt; T</code></td><td>returns what it accepts, throws a sentence at what it refuses</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>StandardSchemaV1&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>the interop spec zod, valibot and arktype all answer to</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A JSON Schema document is the <strong>native</strong> shape, and it enters as the
        Standard form.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A JSON Schema document gates a value through `jsonSchema`">
        <Example
            id="json-schema"
            title="`jsonSchema(document)` — a JSON Schema document as a gate"
            summary="The seats on a deal, capped at fifty by a JSON Schema document. Press + past the cap and the deal keeps what it had."
            files={{
                'lib/Seats.abide': seatsSource,
            }}
        >
            <Seats />
        </Example>

        <p><code>jsonSchema(document)</code> wraps a document as a Standard Schema that carries its
        own validator, so it goes wherever a <code>Schema</code> goes. The refusal is worded by the
        document, and keyed the way <code>Issues&lt;T&gt;</code> keys any other schema's.</p>

        <p>A handler means <code>JsonSchema</code>, and it is the only shape publishable to a tool
        definition or an OpenAPI operation — the wrapper exposes the document where a Standard
        Schema exposes its JSON Schema, so it is published as written.</p>

        <p>A document is data, so something has to read it, and <code>jsonSchema</code> is what
        brings the validator. A page that never calls it carries none of it (19.13). Hand a gate a
        bare document and it refuses with the sentence naming the wrapper (19.14), rather than
        accepting a shape it never checked.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`Issues<T>` is keyed by path, not a list">
        <Example
            id="schema-issues"
            title="`Issues<T>` — a message under the field it belongs to"
            summary="The invoice search. Empty the query, or ask for four — each message lands under its own field."
            files={{
                'lib/InvoiceSearch.abide': invoiceSearchSource,
            }}
        >
            <InvoiceSearch />
        </Example>

        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`type Issues<T> = T extends object
    ? Partial<Record<Paths<T> | '', string[]>>
    : string[]`} />

        <p>A record on a composite, a bare list on a primitive — because the reader always has the
        path in hand. A flat list would cost a <code>find</code> <strong>per field per
        render</strong> — twenty scans on a twenty-field form. It is <code>string[]</code> and not
        <code>string</code> because one field fails two ways at once, too short <em>and</em>
        malformed, and one message per path is a loss with nothing naming it.</p>

        <p><code>Paths&lt;T&gt;</code> is the dot-joined leaf paths — <code>lines.0.qty</code>,
        never <code>lines[0].qty</code>. One spelling, and it is the one every Standard Schema
        already hands back: <code>['lines', 0, 'qty']</code> joined, so there is no bracket grammar
        to specify and nothing to parse back.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Key</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>a field</td><td><code>'lines.0.qty'</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>the whole value — a <code>refine</code>, a thrown sentence</td><td><code>''</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>a primitive</td><td>no key; <code>data</code> is the <code>string[]</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Two costs worth knowing. <code>Paths&lt;T&gt;</code> is <strong>depth-limited</strong>,
        because a recursive template-literal type is typecheck time paid on every handler; past the
        limit the key widens to <code>string</code> and the lookup stops being checked. And
        integer-like keys iterate first in JavaScript whatever the insertion order — what an array
        wants, and what a mixed shape merely has.</p>

        <p>Read on:
        <a href={url('/machines/describe-your-api-without-writing-a-spec')}>OpenAPI</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`validateJson` checks a value nothing gates">
        <Example
            id="validate-json"
            title="`validateJson(schema, value)` — the issues, with no value to gate"
            summary="A contact row pasted from a partner's export, checked before it is imported. Fix the plan and the seats, and watch each issue leave its own field."
            files={{
                'lib/PastedRow.abide': pastedRowSource,
            }}
        >
            <PastedRow />
        </Example>

        <p><code>validateJson(schema, value)</code> is the validator <code>jsonSchema</code>
        carries, called on its own. It hands back <code>null</code> when the value matches and
        <code>Issues&lt;T&gt;</code> when it does not, and stores and refuses nothing either way.
        Reach for it where the value is not one you hold yet — a pasted row, an import, a file — and
        the only question is whether it fits.</p>

        <p>A keyword it does not implement throws rather than being skipped, because a document
        declaring a constraint nothing applied reads as checked. <code>format</code> is one.
        <code>title</code>, <code>description</code> and <code>default</code> are annotations, and
        pass.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`JsonValue` is the bound on an argument">
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`type JsonValue =
    | null
    | boolean
    | number
    | string
    | JsonValue[]
    | { [k: string]: JsonValue }`} />

        <p>The bound exists because the canonical wire form of an arguments object is also its
        <strong>cache key</strong>. A value that cannot be serialized cannot be keyed, and a keyed
        value that cannot key its arguments cannot coalesce two callers onto one load.</p>

        <p><code>GET</code> and <code>DELETE</code> narrow it further, their wire form being URL
        parameters: flat only, an array being the repeated key <code>URLSearchParams</code> already
        has a form for. A nested object is keyable and is not sendable, so it is refused at compile
        time naming the method — and the error says the fix is a <code>POST</code>, and that a
        <code>POST</code> gives up the browser cache.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/change-something-on-the-server')}>Mutations</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Coercion happens before validation, not inside the schema">
        <p>A <code>GET</code>'s arguments arrive as text. They are coerced from the JSON Schema's
        declared <code>type</code> first — <code>"number"</code> through <code>Number()</code>,
        <code>"boolean"</code> from <code>"true"</code> / <code>"false"</code>, <code>"array"</code>
        from the repeated key — and validated after:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Declared</th><th><code>?limit=20</code> becomes</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>number</code></td><td><code>20</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>array</code></td><td>every repeat of the key, in order</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>That table is total over the flat argument types, and it is the same one <code>style:
        form, explode: true</code> publishes. It runs at the first <code>ctx.args()</code> that
        asks, so every rung after that and the handler see the coerced value.</p>

        <p><code>validateJson</code> stays a checker. Putting coercion in the schema instead would
        ask every form of <code>Schema</code> to coerce, which the native validator does not do and
        a Standard Schema does its own way.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A schema refusal is 422 and carries the issues">
        <p>A schema refusal is <code>422</code> carrying <code>Failed&lt;'ValidationError',
        Issues&lt;Args&gt;&gt;</code>. A malformed body — not valid JSON at all — is
        <code>400</code>. Both are raised where the first middleware rung asks for the
        arguments.</p>

        <p><code>isError</code> narrows <code>.data</code> to <code>Issues&lt;Args&gt;</code>, so
        the key is checked against your own argument shape rather than being an untyped string index
        — a typo in <code>'lines.0.qty'</code> is a compile error.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`args` refuses; it does not decide the key">
        <Example
            id="schema-key"
            title={'`reactive(fn, { args })` — the gate refuses, the arguments key'}
            summary="One invoice, spelled two ways. Open the second spelling, then go back to the first."
            files={{
                'lib/InvoiceKey.abide': invoiceKeySource,
            }}
        >
            <InvoiceKey />
        </Example>

        <p>A keyed value's entry is filed under the arguments <strong>as sent</strong>, and
        <code>args</code> runs after (11.26). A normalization in it reaches the body (11.68) but
        never changes which entry it is (11.28).</p>

        <p>The order is forced rather than chosen. A browser has to compute the same key the server
        does in order to find its seeded value, and it cannot run a gate it was never sent. So the
        key is taken before validation. Otherwise every handler with a normalizing gate re-issues
        its call on hydration.</p>

        <p>In channels keyed by their args the same cost is sharper: two spellings of a channel key
        are <strong>two channels</strong>, and a message patched into one is invisible to a
        subscriber of the other with nothing warning either. Where arguments must collapse,
        normalize them at the caller or type them so the variation cannot be spelled. The gate is
        the wrong instrument.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A channel gates the array with `schema` and the key with `args`">
        <p>A channel is an array, so its <code>schema</code> checks the whole array on every
        <code>set</code> — the app's own included, because validity is not a question about who is
        asking:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="src/lib/channels.ts — excerpt" code={`export const announcements = reactive<Post[]>([], {
    schema: z.array(messageSchema),
    global: true,
})`} />

        <p>What it does not see is a <code>patch</code>. An append through <code>patch</code>
        mutates the array in place rather than minting a production (5.7), so neither
        <code>schema</code> nor <code>transform</code> runs on it. A message that has to be checked
        arrives one of two ways: as a <code>set</code> of the whole array, where <code>schema</code>
        or a <code>transform</code> reads its last element, or checked by the code that patches it
        in. A caller outside the process is always the second kind. Its only way in is a
        <code>POST</code> whose handler patches the channel, so that handler's <code>args</code> and
        its body are the check.</p>

        <p>A channel keyed by its args also has a <strong>channel key</strong>, and that is the
        parameter its body declares, so it is <code>args</code>. The key picks which channel while
        the messages flow:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="src/lib/channels.ts — excerpt" code={`export const thread = reactive((_args: { id: string }): Post[] => [], {
    args: z.object({ id: z.string().min(1) }),
    schema: z.array(messageSchema),
    global: true,
})`} />

        <p>Two positions, so two options. Both are derived from the type when not provided, the same
        way a handler's are, and a channel key is checked but never normalized (11.28): two
        spellings of one key are two channels.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> — why a 401 rung
            runs before any parse</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/put-a-ceiling-on-a-request')}>Limits</a> — the ceiling on a
            body, checked before buffering</li>
            <li><a href={url('/machines/describe-your-api-without-writing-a-spec')}>OpenAPI</a> —
            where the schema is published</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
